
==============================================================================
[1] WHAT phi^U EXPANDS INTO: spanning trees, and how the block product sharpens it
==============================================================================
phi^U(Gamma) = Ursell / Mayer coefficient of the plaquette family Gamma:
   phi^U(G) = sum_{A subset E(G_Gamma), (Gamma,A) connected} (-1)^{|A|}                (1.1)
  (i)  cumulant reading: partition-lattice Moebius sum over connected multigraphs, signs +-
  (ii) Penrose / Brydges-Kennedy tree interpolation: reorganised as a sum over SPANNING
       TREES of the intersection graph G_Gamma,  |phi^U| <= tau(G_Gamma)   (Penrose)   (1.2)
  (iii) BLOCK FACTORISATION (new here): (1.1) is multiplicative over 2-connected blocks,
       because (V,A) is connected  <=>  A contains every bridge AND A restricted to each
       block is connected & spanning.  Hence, with mu(B) := sum_{A c.s. in B}(-1)^{|A|},
         phi^U(G) = (-1)^{#bridges} * prod_{blocks B} mu(B)                       (1.3)
       Consequences:  tree / single edge  -> |mu|=1 ;  K_n -> |mu|=(n-1)! ;  C_n -> n-1.
       A link carries exactly 6 plaquettes  =>  clique number of the plaquette graph = 6
       => the factorial growth of |mu| STOPS at n=6.  This is the structural reason a
          per-cluster Cbar cannot run away, and why tau >> |phi^U| on dense clusters.  [T]

==============================================================================
[2] EXACT |phi^U| AND tau FOR THE CLIQUE FAMILY  (bitmask enumeration, exact integers)
==============================================================================
 the k plaquettes through ONE LINK form K_k (k<=6) -- the only way to get a clique
  n=1 |E|= 0  phi^U=1      tau=1       |phi^U|^{1/n}=1.0       tau^{1/n}=1.0
  n=2 |E|= 1  phi^U=-1     tau=1       |phi^U|^{1/n}=1.0       tau^{1/n}=1.0
  n=3 |E|= 3  phi^U=2      tau=3       |phi^U|^{1/n}=1.259921  tau^{1/n}=1.4422496
  n=4 |E|= 6  phi^U=-6     tau=16      |phi^U|^{1/n}=1.5650846 tau^{1/n}=2.0
  n=5 |E|=10  phi^U=24     tau=125     |phi^U|^{1/n}=1.888175  tau^{1/n}=2.6265278
  n=6 |E|=15  phi^U=-120   tau=1296    |phi^U|^{1/n}=2.2209062 tau^{1/n}=3.3019272
  => per-cluster Cbar >= (n-1)!^{1/n} up to n=6:  1, 1, 1.2599, 1.5651, 1.8882, 2.2209
     (K_6 exact: phi^U=-120, tau=1296 -> the tau bound over-counts by 10.8x at n=6)
     and it STOPS: no K_n for n>=7 (clique number 6)  => per-cluster sup is ~2.22, not factorial.
     control (the 6 plaquettes at a corner are NOT a clique): |E|=12 phi^U=-64 tau=384

==============================================================================
[3] NO-GO FOR THE tau ROUTE: the periodic 4D box is 20-regular and tau^{1/96} >> 3.5
==============================================================================
 L=2 periodic 4D box: n=96 plaquettes, 4*16=64 links, |E|(simple)=864, degree=18..18
   (the infinite lattice would give 20*96/2=960 pairs; on the L=2 torus some pairs share
    TWO links and collapse in the simple graph: 96 pairs lost, deg=18 regular)
 tau = 3.35591544059935629510374e+116   (exact integer; Bareiss, 0.1 s)
 tau^{1/n} = 16.36102587      (AM-GM envelope Delta = 20)
 => sup_Gamma tau(G_Gamma)^{1/n} >= 16.361 >> 3.5.
    Therefore: NO refinement of Kirchhoff/AM-GM can ever deliver a PER-CLUSTER
    Cbar <= 3.5 through (1.2). The looseness is not in the algebra, it is in using tau
    (i.e. position entropy) as the counting device: tau pays for positions again.   [T] no-go

==============================================================================
[4] WHERE THE <=20 BREAKS: position entropy e*Delta is already charged inside ubar
==============================================================================
 ubar := e*Delta*Cbar*u = (KP local sum). Its e*Delta factor is the positioned-tree count:
   # rooted trees at p with n vertices, max degree Delta  <=  (e*Delta)^n  =  Delta^n * e^n
   Delta^n  = positions  (already paid by e*Delta in ubar)
   e^n      = labelled-tree entropy / n!  (Cayley + Stirling) -- see the table below:
      n      (n^{n-2})^{1/n}   (n^{n-2}/n!)^{1/n}
    5      2.6265278        1.0081978
    20     14.822689        1.7849283
    100    91.201084        2.4004903
    500    487.724          2.6302759
    1000   986.27949        2.6692875
      inf    n/e -> inf        e = 2.7182818
 => the correct split is  Cbar <= e = 2.71828  (shape entropy only), NOT Delta = 20;
    charging Cbar=Delta pays the SAME position entropy twice. a01's 20 is an upper
    bound for the wrong object (per-cluster tau^{1/n}), not for the Cbar in ubar.      [T]
 pessimistic variant (count rooted unlabelled SHAPES, A000081, growth = Otter constant):
   n   :       1       2       3       4       5       6       7       8
   a_n :       1       1       2       4       9      20      48     115
   ^1/n:     1.0     1.0  1.2599  1.4142  1.5518  1.6475  1.7385  1.8096
   Otter = 2.9557658  < 3.5  =>  the bound is robust to which of the two splits one uses.

